In article <25410@cs.yale.edu> beigel-richard@CS.YALE.EDU (Richard Beigel) writes:
>
>I really like Steve's suggestion.  Making shift-move go only one square
>is a lovely idea.  As for ctrl-shift-move, it doesn't exist in ascii, so
>my datamedia (remember them?) won't send it.  Perhaps that's a feature.  :)

Thanks! ;^).  And yes, it is true, there is no such thing as ctrl-shft-char,
as several people have nicely informed me in email ;^).  But that's OK,
the *idea* is the point.  Just make it meta-move or some such.  But sorry
for the unimplementable implementation ;^).

Here are some of the reasons I think this is a good idea:

o Avoids cheapening the game with confirmations, while accomplishing much
  the same effect when moving normally - "you stop to avoid the pool"
  or, on <shft>-move, "You dive into the pool" - or "You leap down the
  trap door", etc. (assuming that you have already found the trap door
  in advance).

o Avoids accidentally attacking a creature you had no intention of attacking.
  To me this really makes sense.  Everyone knows the difference between
  walking around and attacking someone.  I would not absent-mindedly
  impale a creature - that is a quite distinct action from walking up to
  it.

o "You stop to avoid hitting the black dragon  --more--" (ouch!)  You now
  would have to consciously attack creatures.  Attempting to walk into them
  would give them a free swipe at you.  So there could actually be a new
  sense of danger in battle.  This is a good thing, to me.

o Using the shifted character instead of toggling a mode would be more
  intuitive to me.  I do not want to double the number of keystrokes I
  have to use in a battle.  And it is too easy to forget which mode you
  are toggled into.  I have enough trouble remembering if I have pickup
  toggled on or off ;^).  And upper-case somehow *seems* more assertive
  to me.  So this idea appeals to my sense of "rightness".

o This "aggressive movement" could be extended to all sorts of things.
  <shft>-move into a closed/locked door would be interpreted as an attempt
  to force the door open.  <shft>-move into a wall would result in
  "you ram your forehead into the brick wall  --more--", possibly
  knocking you unconscious.  Perhaps only an aggressive move would
  actually succeed in moving a boulder: "You stop to avoid the boulder."
  Other possibilities: "You run into the statue - You trip over the
  statue - The statue shatters on the ground - You are stunned."

o There would still be a danger of falling into a pool if you were, for
  example, battling eels, since you would be in the middle of a bunch
  of pools and using <shft>-move.

Well, that is all the advantages I could think of immediately.  What
bothers you about this idea?  Or what do you like about it?

